From 4952c6662d5ed3e4d5298d30682502c9fe8edcb1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael P Jones <QuixoticSeven@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:33:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] fm1-core: the wasm JIT compiles the FPU, float if-blocks
 and calls through a register

Profiled in the browser build (V8's profiler, the JIT's own helper counts),
three kinds of instruction kept busy firmwares out of compiled code:

- every FPU op (`3f e5`: + - * /, the multiply-accumulates, ftoi/ftou,
  itof/utof) was a helper call out of the compiled region: ~18 M a second
  in Melodee 1.4.4 with a chord held, on both cores;
- an `iff (R op R) {` block with a float compare was a helper call, and an
  if-else one was not compiled at all (its then-part ran interpreted);
- `call R` and `goto R` had no micro-op: each went through `step` and
  `exec_syntax`, and ended the compiled run (Melodee's UI calls through
  function pointers, ~2 M a second).

The FPU ops now compile to the wasm float instructions `fpu_d` compiles to
(f32.add/sub/mul/div, mul then add/sub for ops 7 and 8, the saturating
truncations, the conversions), so the results are the same bits. fmin and
fmax (Rust's NaN rules) and unknown unary codes stay with the helper. The
if-blocks use `cmp`'s float compares for families 8 and up, as `cmp_cond`
does, and an if-else block's then-part now comes along for any family.
`call R` / `goto R` are micro-ops (CallR / GotoR, the arms of exec_syntax):
they end a block, and compiled code goes on through the region's jump
table as a return does. The AArch64 JIT runs the two new micro-ops through
its helper.

Melodee 1.4.4 in Node at 360 MHz: 0.97 -> 0.69 of real time. X0X 1.1.0
0.60 -> 0.58, Felucca 1.6.1, ChoralRoot 0.25.1 unchanged to slightly
faster. Screens and audio are identical to before for the single-core
firmwares; Melodee's audio differs a little from the second second of a
chord on: cpu1 runs after each 1024-instruction slice of cpu0, and the
slices now end at other block boundaries (with this change Melodee's
compiled and interpreted runs agree, which they did not before).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
